PUBLIC RESPONSE: The most direct way to block p-coumarate catabolism in C. glutamicum is to delete pobA (cg1226), which encodes 4-hydroxybenzoate 3-hydroxylase. p-Coumarate is converted to 4-hydroxybenzoate; PobA then hydroxylates it to protocatechuate, feeding the downstream β-ketoadipate pathway. Removing pobA therefore traps the molecule as 4-hydroxybenzoate and prevents further degradation while leaving the central metabolism of the host largely intact.

(The same logic applies to the ferulate/p-coumarate CoA-ligase/hydratase/lyase genes (phdA–E), but pobA is the single, most commonly targeted knockout reported for this purpose.)

}

